            text: "Theranos, a company that was once valued at nine billion dollars on the strength of a blood-testing device that barely worked, offers a case study in institutional failure that few business schools have managed to resist teaching. Elizabeth Holmes, whose charisma persuaded a board stacked with former secretaries of state and retired generals rather than practising scientists, ran the company for over a decade before regulators and journalists, not insiders, finally exposed how little the technology could actually do. The board, which included figures whose expertise lay chiefly in diplomacy and the military rather than laboratory medicine, exercised almost no meaningful oversight over the one claim, that the device worked, on which the entire company's value depended.\n\nWhat makes Theranos instructive rather than merely sensational is how ordinary its governance failures were. Patronage, the tendency to reward loyalty and personal connection over independent judgement, shaped the composition of the board from the outset, producing a group of directors, several of whom later admitted they had never personally reviewed the underlying science, who were structurally unlikely to challenge a founder they had been recruited, in effect, to admire. Checks and balances, the institutional mechanisms meant to prevent exactly this kind of concentration of unverified authority, existed on paper, an audit committee, a scientific advisory arrangement, without functioning in any meaningfully independent way.\n\nScrutiny, when it eventually arrived, came almost entirely from outside the company's own formal structures: a newspaper investigation, built on sources inside the firm who risked their careers to speak, did more to expose the fraud than any internal mechanism designed for that exact purpose. This pattern recurs with striking regularity across institutional scandals, Volkswagen's emissions fraud and FIFA's decades of World Cup bidding corruption among them, where internal oversight bodies, whose formal purpose was to catch precisely this sort of wrongdoing, failed to do so until external pressure made continued denial impossible.\n\nNepotism and patronage are not identical, though they frequently travel together: nepotism rewards family or personal ties specifically, while patronage more broadly rewards loyalty and connection regardless of blood relation, and institutions entrenched in either pattern tend to develop boards, and more broadly leadership cultures, selected for agreeableness rather than independence. Once such a culture becomes sufficiently entrenched, internal dissent carries a real cost to the dissenter's career, which quietly discourages precisely the kind of scrutiny an institution most needs when something has gone wrong.\n\nNone of this implies that outside scrutiny alone is sufficient, or that formal checks and balances are worthless; well-designed oversight bodies, genuinely independent audit committees among them, have caught fraud early in plenty of less famous cases that never made headlines precisely because the system worked as intended. What Theranos and its counterparts do suggest is that accountability, to function as more than a word on a corporate website, requires structures specifically designed to reward uncomfortable questions, a design feature strikingly absent from most boardrooms built instead to reward consensus and personal trust."
